    abstract:
      "This chapter examines the emerging problem of childhood obesity in India within a context where undernutrition, stunting, anaemia, and food insecurity continue to affect large sections of the population. It argues that child obesity should not be understood simply as a consequence of affluence, but as a structural outcome of India’s uneven socioeconomic and nutritional transition. Changes in food systems, increasing availability of inexpensive energy-dense and nutrient-poor foods, urbanisation, sedentary lifestyles, and unequal access to nutritious food and safe environments have contributed to the coexistence of obesity and deprivation. The chapter highlights regional and socioeconomic variations in childhood obesity and examines how nutritional inequality shapes these outcomes. It further argues that India’s nutrition policies have historically focused on undernutrition while giving insufficient attention to excess weight and the double burden of malnutrition. Understanding childhood obesity through the lens of structural inequality and uneven development is therefore essential for developing more comprehensive nutrition policies.",
