  "intro": [
    "Gender is a major factor in determining economic rewards, decision-making power, labor division, and resource access in agrarian communities. The term “Sex” and “Gender” are frequently used synonymously, they are conceptually different notions that serve as the basis for comprehending gender-based disparities in economic systems, including agriculture. The biological and physiological traits that set males and females apart are referred to as sex. The roles, attitudes, duties and power dynamics that societies assign to men and women referred to as gender. In developing nations, one of the main sources of employment, especially in rural areas, is the agri-food system. Both men and women participate in food systems, but the nature and extent of their participation varies depending on the structure of the economy and gender norms. Women play important roles at different nodes of both agricultural and off-farm value chains, but in many countries their contributions are either underestimated or limited by prevailing societal norms or gender-specific barriers (Quisumbing et al. 2021).Women are actively involved in a range of roles from production and processing to retailing and consumption; they grow and manage crops, tend livestock, work in agribusinesses and food retailing, prepare food for their families, and much more (Malapit et al., 2020). However, women's contributions to food systems are frequently overlooked or underappreciated and when women are not acknowledged as \"real farmers,\" their environmental and farming endeavors are either hidden or seen as an extension of domestic duties. Women still face persistent economic disadvantages, most notably the gender pay gap, which is defined as the systematic difference in earnings between men and women performing comparable work or contributing similar labor inputs. According to estimates from the International Labour Organization's Global Wage Report 2018/19, women earn approximately 20 percent less than men across the world, with larger median gender wage gaps observed in lower-income countries (ILO, 2018). In the agri-food system, this gap is frequently wider than in non-agricultural sectors due to structural inequalities, informality of work, and deeply ingrained socio-cultural norms. In order to",
    "achieve gender equality among lower-income rural households, it is imperative to address gender wage discrepancies in agricultural work. The gender pay gap in agriculture is a reflection of larger differences in access to productive resources, education, skills, technology, and decision-making authority rather than just unequal salaries. Because of these differences, women's productivity and negotiating power are restricted, which perpetuates cycles of poverty and food insecurity. Therefore, closing the gender pay gap is essential for attaining inclusive agricultural growth, enhancing rural livelihoods and fulfilling international commitments like the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), especially SDG 1 (No Poverty), SDG 2 (Zero Hunger), SDG 5 (Gender Equality) and SDG 8 (Decent Work and Economic Growth). However, it is important to understand the gap and estimate the share due to gender discrimination and further frame policies aimed at tackling the gender wage gap. In order to develop gender-equitable labor market policies, it is crucial to comprehend the factors that contribute to the gender wage gap and the role that gender-based discrimination plays at various stages of structural development. In this chapter, the factors responsible for the gender wage gaps and policies are discussed that need to be embedded within food systems for women empowerment and gender equality."
  ],
