        "to multi-national corporations, further eroding women's traditional knowledge and control. While women currently perform 61.00 percent of all farm work, they are often rendered \"invisible\" by statistics that categorize them merely as housewives. It was not until the Tenth Plan (2002–2007) that policy explicitly shifted from welfare toward empowerment. (Bollikonda et al., 2017) Between 2010 and 2025, the status of women in Indian agriculture underwent a significant shift characterized by a \"forced back\" migration to cultivation, especially during the 2020–2022 pandemic- related economic slowdown. While the 2011 Census reported that nearly every second agricultural laborer was a woman, by 2022, women represented 62.80 percent of the total agricultural workforce, compared to only 38.10 percent of men. This trend of \"feminization of agriculture\" continues in modern contexts like Nepal, where women now represent 50.90 percent of the agricultural population",
        "due to ongoing male migration for non-farm jobs, leaving women to manage food security while carrying a \"triple burden\" of production, reproduction and exploitive societal norms. This \"feminization of agriculture\" is primarily driven by male out-migration for non-farm jobs, leaving women to manage food security while carrying a \"triple burden\" of production, reproduction and exploitive societal norms. Senapati and Mukhopadhyay (2024) stated that despite their dominance, women face persistent structural inequality. In 2023, the gender pay-gap remained high at 27.00 percent, with women earning significantly less than men for similar labor. Furthermore, during 2018– 2022, female self-employed workers experienced a higher negative rate of earnings growth compared to their male counterparts. While initiatives like Self-Help Groups (SHGs) and the National Rural Livelihoods Mission have improved access to credit, women still struggle with limited land ownership and a lack of decision-making power, meaning true empowerment in the sector is still a work in progress. Despite these advancements, the legacy of women as \"silent heroes\" persists, as they continue to perform the bulk of agricultural labor worldwide while often lacking the same institutional support as their male counterparts."
