      "heading": "3. Conceptual Understanding: Gender Pay Gap in the Agri-Food System",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The Gender Pay Gap is the systematic difference between the average earnings of men and women, with global estimates from the International Labour Organization indicating that women earn approximately 20.00 percent less than men. According to the Benali 2025, the gender pay gap can be systematically examined using wage decomposition techniques like the Kitagawa–Oaxaca–Blinder (KOB) method (Blinder, 1973; Kitagawa, 1955; Oaxaca, 1973),which separates observed wage differentials into two core components. The first is the endowment (explained) Effects which captures the share of the wage gap caused by differences in observable characteristics between men and women, such as education levels, age, work experience, and household composition.The second is the structural (unexplained) effect, which represents the portion of the gap that persists even when men and women possess similar characteristics. This unexplained component reflects unequal returns to comparable endowments such as women receiving lower wages than men with the same educational attainment and is commonly interpreted as evidence of labor-market discrimination or gender-biased institutional structures. In the specific context of the agri-food system this gap reflects a \"wedge\" between the marginal product of labor and the actual market wage received, a disparity that the sources indicate is generally larger",
        "for women than for men. Agriculture is very often portrayed as and perceived to be a masculine activity (Elias et al., 2018). Women's labor engagement is significant but underappreciated in the agri-food system, which includes a wide range of activities such as agricultural production, livestock rearing, fisheries, post-harvest management, agro-processing, marketing, and food distribution. It is commonly stated as women earning less per hour or unit of work than males.The disparity between male and female earnings can be expressed either in absolute terms (difference in daily or monthly income) or in relative terms (% difference). It is crucial to evaluate the magnitude of gender wage disparities and comprehend the factors that influence them at various phases of economic development. The gender pay gap is like two people running the same distance on a track, but one runner is assigned to a lane filled with invisible hurdles. Even if both runners have the same training and stamina (endowments), the runner in the lane with hurdles will always finish with a slower time and a smaller reward (structural discrimination) because the track itself was not built to be equal. This variation is particularly evident across the agri-food system, which is broadly divided into primary agriculture, the wider agri-food system encompassing processing and services, and sectors outside the agri-food system, with gender pay gaps varying distinctly across these stages. In primary agriculture, particularly in developing countries such as India,women often work as invisible or casualized laborers, resulting in a gender pay gap of roughly 27.00 percent as of 2023. According to a periodic labour survey report (2024), a similar pattern is observed in Malawi, where rural female wages were found to be 35.00 percent lower than males. However, evidence from the lowest-income countries, including Ethiopia and Uganda, suggests that gender wage gaps in primary agriculture are sometimes statistically insignificant or lower than those in middle-income economies, a pattern that changes as countries undergo structural transformation and labor markets become more differentiated (Palacios et al., 2015). As the employment shifts beyond the farm into a broader Agri-food system covering food processing, manufacturing, and related services the gender wage gap tends to widen further. At this stage, both endowment effects, such as disparities in education and work experience, and structural effects, including discrimination and unequal returns to labor, play nearly equal roles in sustaining wage inequalities. In contrast, sectors outside the agri-food system generally exhibit smaller gender pay gaps, largely driven by differences in human capital rather than overt structural disadvantages. As economies move toward more formal, skill-intensive employment, the mechanisms underlying wage gaps increasingly differ from those observed in the informal and labor-intensive agricultural settings typical of developing countries."
      ]
