          "text": "                                                           Law, Diplomacy, Tech & Policy Review\n                                                           Volume 1\n                                                           Issue 1\n                                                           Page (20-60)\n\n\n\n\n                              Figure-2 Gender pay gap scenario in Africa\n\nAs shown in Figure-2 the difference across wages shows that the productivity gap, which reflects\ndisparities in efficiency, technology, and resource utilization, is the difference in output generated per\nworker or per unit of input between sectors, regions, or groups. On the other hand, the wage gap, which\nis impacted by labor market institutions, abilities, and social issues like discrimination, indicates\ndisparities in workers' earnings even when their productivity may be comparable. Africa reflects the\nstory of women’s earnings compared to men’s is one of persistent inequality, though the reasons shift\nfrom place to place. In East and Southern Africa, for example, women often bring home less money\nthan men, earning about 81 cents for every dollar, largely because they spend fewer hours in paid work\n(UN Women, 2023). On farms, the gap feels even sharper. In Ethiopia, women’s productivity is 24%\nlower, mainly because they cannot rely on male family labour, and experts say that closing this gap\ncould lift more than a million people out of poverty (UNDP, 2018). Kenya offers a brighter picture,\nwith one of the smallest gaps at just 8%, but in Tanzania and Malawi, the lack of male labour support\nexplains much of the inequality, nearly half of Malawi’s gap is tied to this single factor (UN Women\nPolicy Brief No. 11, 2019). Rwanda too struggles with differences in agricultural productivity (UN\nWomen, 2015), while Mozambique shows a different twist: once worker characteristics are taken into\naccount, the pay gap almost disappears (UN Women, 2023). Other countries, like Namibia and\n\n\n\n29 | P a g e"
