          "text": "                                                         Law, Diplomacy, Tech & Policy Review\n                                                         Volume 1\n                                                         Issue 1\n                                                         Page (20-60)\n\n\n\n\n5.5 Perspectives from Europe\n\n\n\n\n                            Figure-5 Gender pay gap scenario in Europe\n\nIn Europe, the gender pay gap paints a mixed picture, with some countries showing persistent\ninequality while others have nearly closed or even reversed the gap. Germany remains among the\nhighest in the EU, with women earning 18% less than men in 2023 and 16% less in 2024 (Eurostat,\nGerman Federal Statistical Office, 2024). Austria and Hungary also report gaps above 17%, while\nEstonia consistently records the highest in the EU at around 21% (Eurostat, 2022–2023). Latvia\nfollows closely with a 19% gap (Eurostat, 2024). On the other hand, several countries demonstrate\nmuch smaller disparities: Belgium shows only a 1% overall gap, and in some cases women working\nfull-time earn more than men (Eurostat, 2023). Italy’s gap is just 2%, while Poland, Romania, and\nSlovenia each report gaps below 5% (Eurostat, 2023). Luxembourg even shows a slight reverse gap,\nwith women earning marginally more than men on average (Eurostat, 2022–2023). Overall, while\nCentral and Eastern European countries like Estonia and Latvia struggle with some of the widest gaps,\nWestern and Southern European nations such as Belgium, Italy, and Luxembourg illustrate that\n\n\n\n\n33 | P a g e"
