          "type": "text",
          "text": "                                                            Law, Diplomacy, Tech & Policy Review\n                                                            Volume 1\n                                                            Issue 1\n                                                            Page (20-60)\n\n\n\n   Yojana                                                             1.2M        limited\n(DDU-GKY)                                                            trained     agri focus\n   Periodic        2017        Gender-     Statistic    MOSPI          N/A       Evidence       MOSPI,\nLabour Force      (annual)    disaggreg        al                                 base for      Annual\n   Survey                        ated      framew                                 policies,\n   (PLFS)                       labour        ork                                wage gap\n                              force data                                        documenta\n                                                                                    tion\nState-Specific   Ongoing         Sets      Agricult       State       Varies       State-         State\n Agricultural                 minimum        ural        labour      by state    dependent       Govts.\n    Wage                        daily      labourer      depts.,                enforceme\nNotifications                 wages by         s         district                    nt,\n                             gender/tas                collectors                persistent\n                                  k                                                 gaps\n\n\n\n9. Conclusion\n\nThis chapter provided a holistic understanding of the gender pay gap in agriculture by tracing its\nhistorical roots, conceptual foundations, and contemporary manifestations across both developed and\ndeveloping economies. By examining the evolution of women's roles in farming from subsistence\ncontributors to active economic agents        the chapter establishes that their labor has long been\nundervalued and underrecognized, a pattern deeply embedded in social, cultural, and structural\nrealities. The comparative analysis between developed and developing countries reveals that while the\ngap is universal, its depth and drivers vary significantly. In developing nations like India, it is\ncompounded by caste hierarchies, land ownership disparities, limited education access, and informal\nemployment norms, whereas in developed economies, occupational segregation and work-life balance\npenalties remain dominant forces. Despite their crucial role in agricultural production and the growing\nfeminization of the industry, women are still disproportionately employed in low-wage, unpaid, and\ninformal jobs with little access to resources, skills and decision-making authority. Instead of disparities\nin work effort alone, institutional gender inequality and discrimination are the primary cause of the\nwage disparity in the agri-food industry. Women are paid less because they are more likely to work in\n\n\n\n\n49 | P a g e"
