          "“The pandemic is a stark reminder that our fates are interconnected and that no one is secure until everyone is secured.” — The then Hon’ble Prime Minister of Bangladesh, Sheikh Hasina (Sazid, 2022, p. 92)",
          "The COVID era has seen vaccine diplomacy as one of the tools countries used to demonstrate their soft power to other nations. The big countries like India, China, Russia, and the USA have used this instrument to strengthen their bilateral relations with other countries. Thus, vaccine diplomacy became an essential part of global health diplomacy. There have been several studies in different parts of the world that have examined the development and implications of it post COVID 19. According to Sarker & Ghosh (2023), vaccine diplomacy has been defined as a series of actions taken by governments in response to health crises to acquire vaccines, finance, and international cooperation. The May 2022 report recognised Bangladesh as a pioneer in dealing with the COVID outbreak and placed it at the top among the nations with identical socio-economic structure of South Asia (The Financial Express, 2022). In the case of Bangladesh, this tool has played a significant role in acquiring vaccines for a huge population. The study has shown that Bangladesh has taken a proactive diplomatic stance in order to reduce the effects of the pandemic and maintain public health security.",
          "There are several key elements that have influenced the country's diplomacy during the pandemic period. Bangladesh's diplomacy was based on five major components (Sarker & Ghosh, 2023, p. 11): ● Identifying critical intervention areas ● Assessing internal vaccine demand ● Utilising multiple sources of vaccines ● Generating diverse sources of external finance ● Utilising soft power diplomacy. These elements enabled the country to manage the challenges posed by vaccine shortages and global competition for vaccine supplies.",
          "Initially Bangladesh used diplomatic channels to stay close with its powerful Asian neighbours, India and China. Bangladesh was favourable regarding the potential trial of the Chinese vaccine developed by Sinovac, to be tested on 4,200 Bangladeshi volunteers (China’s COVID-19 Vaccine Trial Approved in Bangladesh, 2020). Simultaneously, Bangladesh was connecting with India for the procurement of the Oxford–AstraZeneca vaccine. Bangladesh even offered India to have a vaccine trial inside its territory. However, according to some diplomats this act paved the way for Bangladesh to get secured vaccination (Sarker & Ghosh, 2023, p. 6). India promised Bangladesh to provide the AstraZeneca vaccines once large-scale production started. With the beginning of the Vaccine Maitri campaign, Bangladesh was one of the first countries to get vaccines (Chawla, 2021). Under the bilateral agreement Bangladesh purchased 15 million vaccines from the Serum Institute in March 2022 (Byron and Habib, 2022). As a supplementary action regarding the agreement with India, Bangladesh’s Foreign Minister called his Indian counterpart as the last resort, requesting to send vaccine doses as a gift (Bhuiyan et al., 2021). The minister said that as part of India's \"Neighbourhood First\" policy, Bangladesh accorded high priority to the relationship with India. Subsequently, this gift of 20 lakh doses of Covishield vaccine was the biggest such dispatch by India to any of the neighbours. Furthermore, Bangladesh obtained 300 million doses of vaccines from multiple sources, like UNICEF, which distributed approximately 190 million doses to Bangladesh through the COVAX, plus it included purchased, received, and donated vaccines too (12.94 Lakh People Receive Booster Dose on 1st Day of Campaign, 2022).",
          "Bangladesh provides a significant case study for understanding how smaller states navigate global vaccine diplomacy. Unlike major powers that produce vaccines domestically, Bangladesh relied largely on international cooperation and diplomatic negotiations to secure vaccine supplies. The other remarkable aspect of vaccine diplomacy in Bangladesh is its engagement in multilateral approaches, including the COVAX facility."
