          "This study adopts a qualitative case-study approach to analyse how Bangladesh navigated India-China geopolitical competition through vaccine diplomacy during the COVID-19 pandemic. Process tracing is employed as the principal method to analyze specific diplomatic moves, agreements, and supply chains that influenced Bangladesh's foreign policy behavior from 2020 to 2022. The unit of analysis comprises vaccine -focused diplomatic engagements with Bangladesh, India, China, and international organi zations like COVAX, including bilateral vaccine agreements, diplomatic declarations, procurement discussions, donation pledges, export disruptions, and technology transfer talks. The study relies on both primary and secondary sources. Primary materials include official government statements, speeches by political leaders, Ministry of External Affairs releases, WHO publications, and bilateral agreements. Secondary sources include peer -reviewed journal articles, policy reports, ORF analyses, vaccination datasets, and selected newspaper coverage relevant to South Asian vaccine diplomacy. Data collection sources are chosen according to the following standards: institutional reliability, relevancy to Bangladesh's vaccine diplomacy, and the empirical verifiability of claims. Unverifiable opinion blogs were avoided unless directly related to diplomatic communications. Data analysis for this research was conducted using thematic analysis on four comparative indicators that included vaccine dosage distributed, pricin g model, delivery schedule, and conditionalities adopted. The study compares India's Vaccine Maitri project with China's strategy of vaccine diplomacy, and it explores the efforts made by Bangladesh to diversify its partnerships amid shifting regional dynamics. The research methodology gives this study the opportunity to conduct vaccine diplomacy analysis not only from the humanitarian aspect but also from the strategic and geopolitics perspective.",
